#N canvas 20 20 1400 760 12;
#X text 20 20 t_noteout -- a stand-in for [noteout] that prints every note event the patch emits \, with the channel it went out on. tools/phase9-assert.sh rewrites [noteout] to [t_noteout] in a SCRATCH COPY \; nothing in Cut It/ is touched and this file is never deployed., f 84;
#X text 20 140 IT CANNOT BE DONE BY SEARCH PATH \, for exactly the reason t_midiout.pd gives: noteout is a BUILT-IN CLASS and Pd resolves the class table before it looks for a file. The box has to be rewritten., f 84;
#X text 20 250 ⚠️ PHASE 6's REWRITE WOULD MISS THIS FILE'S REAL COUNTERPART. Its regex is anchored so the class name must END the line \, which matches a bare [noteout] but NOT [ctlout 123 33]. Phase 9's rewriter takes a trailing argument list \, so this stub must accept creation args too -- hence the f \$1 below rather than a hardcoded default., f 84;
#X text 20 400 creation arg 1: the channel \, exactly as the real object -- [noteout] is channel 1 and [noteout 49] is channel 49. inlet 0: pitch \, HOT. inlet 1: velocity \, cold. inlet 2: channel \, cold. outlets: none. Prints NOTEOUT: <pitch> <velocity> <channel> \, which is inlet order \, so a capture reads the same way the object does., f 84;
#X obj 20 560 inlet;
#X obj 300 560 inlet;
#X obj 580 560 inlet;
#X obj 20 630 pack f f f;
#X obj 20 700 print NOTEOUT;
#X obj 900 480 loadbang;
#X obj 900 530 f \$1;
#X obj 900 580 max 1;
#X text 900 630 THE DEFAULT IS 1 \, NOT 0. A missing creation arg makes f \$1 answer 0 \, and channel 0 is not a channel -- the real object treats a bare [noteout] as channel 1. max 1 is what makes the stub agree with it \, so a rewritten box behaves like the one it replaced whether or not it carried an argument., f 60;
#X connect 4 0 7 0;
#X connect 5 0 7 1;
#X connect 6 0 7 2;
#X connect 7 0 8 0;
#X connect 9 0 10 0;
#X connect 10 0 11 0;
#X connect 11 0 7 2;
